{"name":"Golden Retriever","hex":"#eedec7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Golden Retriever","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A breed of dog, originating in Britain, notable for its golden coat and gentle, affectionate nature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Golden_Retriever"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden Retriever","blocks":[{"text":"The Golden Retriever is a Scottish breed of retriever dog of medium-large size. It is characterised by a gentle and affectionate nature and a striking golden coat. It is a working dog, and registration is subject to successful completion of a working trial. It is commonly kept as a companion dog and is among the most frequently registered breeds in several Western countries; some may compete in dog shows or obedience trials, or work as guide dogs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Golden_Retriever_Dukedestiny01_drvd.jpg/330px-Golden_Retriever_Dukedestiny01_drvd.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Retriever","wikidata":"Q38686"},{"title":"Golden Retriever (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Golden Retriever\" is a song by Super Furry Animals. It was the first single to be issued from the album Phantom Power and reached number 13 on the UK Singles Chart on its release in July 2003. The song is about the relationship between singer Gruff Rhys's girlfriend's two dogs and was written in the same key, with the same guitar tuning and around the same time as several other songs from Phantom Power."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3a/GoldenRetriever-SFA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Retriever_(song)","wikidata":"Q5579731"},{"title":"Retriever","blocks":[{"text":"A retriever is a type of gun dog that retrieves game for a hunter. Generally gun dogs are divided into three major classifications: retrievers, flushing spaniels, and pointing breeds. Retrievers were bred primarily to retrieve birds or other prey and return them to the hunter without damage; retrievers are distinguished in that nonslip retrieval is their primary function. As a result, retriever breeds are bred for soft mouths and a great willingness to please, learn, and obey. A soft mouth refers to the willingness of the dog to carry game in its mouth without biting into it. \"Hard mouth\" is a serious fault in a hunting dog and is very difficult to correct. A hard-mouthed dog renders game unpresentable or at worst inedible."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/MH_Chesapeake.jpg/330px-MH_Chesapeake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retriever","wikidata":"Q39017"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Nous_sitting_with_keeper.jpg/500px-Nous_sitting_with_keeper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Nous_sitting_with_keeper.jpg/500px-Nous_sitting_with_keeper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nous, sitting, at Guisachan, 1870s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANous_sitting_with_keeper.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Mrs_Winifred_Charlesworth.jpg/330px-Mrs_Winifred_Charlesworth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Mrs_Winifred_Charlesworth.jpg/330px-Mrs_Winifred_Charlesworth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Winifred Charlesworth with two of her Golden Retrievers, 1910s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMrs_Winifred_Charlesworth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/At_Sticker_Lumis_Golden.JPG/500px-At_Sticker_Lumis_Golden.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/At_Sticker_Lumis_Golden.JPG/1280px-At_Sticker_Lumis_Golden.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/At_Sticker_Lumis_Golden.JPG/500px-At_Sticker_Lumis_Golden.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Facial features","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAt_Sticker_Lumis_Golden.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Golden_Retrievers_dark_and_light.jpg/500px-Golden_Retrievers_dark_and_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Golden_Retrievers_dark_and_light.jpg/500px-Golden_Retrievers_dark_and_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Differences in coat colours","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGolden_Retrievers_dark_and_light.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Golden_retriever_retrieving_a_pheasant.jpg/500px-Golden_retriever_retrieving_a_pheasant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Golden_retriever_retrieving_a_pheasant.jpg/500px-Golden_retriever_retrieving_a_pheasant.jpg?utm_sou